s  and and that is the history of the  filibuster so we should be very clear  that i believe the president biden wants  to do everything he can  to address racial in inequity in this  country and inequality  and if we are going to deliver on those  promises  we will have to reform the filibuster  yes and to follow up on that question  did the  parliamentarian's decision sort of give  you one issue that you can peg  um ending the filibuster to as opposed  to  maybe the abstract you know the entire  agenda  is there a strategy or conversations to  maybe just hang your company  i don't want a strategy uh  i want us to get rid of the filibuster  because it is too costly to america  the peace in front of us right now is  the minimum wage  but the piece that's coming up is the  voting rights act  and the piece after that is immigration  reform  and another piece is universal child  care  the the uh infrastructure package  is very different if the republicans get  a veto  at every turn through that process  so if we want to deliver on our promises  we got to be willing to get out there  and fight for it and that starts with  getting rid of the filibuster  brendan you want to yeah just said  something real quick because this has  been a pet peeve of mine for a long time  and i have the opportunity  um there's one advantage of having a  philadelphian up on this stage  this very issue was debated at the  constitutional convention in  philadelphia  they considered the idea if you read the  federalist papers in two different  um sightings hamilton and madison talk  about this  they considered the concept of a super  majority and they rejected it  so people who argue i was on a tv show  and a person brought this up oh well you  know you're going against the way it's  always been our founding fathers  nonsense that is complete garbage that  is bad history  the fact of the matter is our nation's  founders were quite smart they were not  perfect  but they were wise they considered 